00:16:41,520 --> 00:16:46,649
which the girl always wore. Her bees and her crows and her wolves were lying in

206
00:16:46,649 --> 00:16:54,059
heaps and drying up, and she had used up all the power of the Golden Cap; but if

207
00:16:54,059 --> 00:16:58,229
she could only get ahold of the Silver Shoes, they would give her more power

208
00:16:58,229 --> 00:17:00,529
than all the other things she had lost.

209
00:17:00,529 --> 00:17:05,520
She watched Dorothy carefully, to see if she ever took off her shoes, thinking she

210
00:17:05,520 --> 00:17:09,658
might steal them. But the child was so proud of her pretty shoes that she never

211
00:17:09,658 --> 00:17:15,569
took them off except at night when she took her bath. The Witch was too much

212
00:17:15,569 --> 00:17:19,349
afraid of the dark to dare go into Dorothy’s room at night to take the

213
00:17:19,349 --> 00:17:24,719
shoes, and her dread of water was greater than her fear of the dark, so she never

214
00:17:24,719 --> 00:17:26,569
came near when Dorothy was bathing.

215
00:17:26,569 --> 00:17:32,210
Indeed, the old Witch never touched water, nor ever let water touch her in any way.

216
00:17:32,210 --> 00:17:37,260
But the wicked creature was very cunning, and she finally thought of a trick that

217
00:17:37,260 --> 00:17:41,039
would give her what she wanted. She placed a bar of iron in the middle of

218
00:17:41,039 --> 00:17:46,170
the kitchen floor, and then by her magic arts made the iron invisible to human

219
00:17:46,170 --> 00:17:50,908
eyes. So that when Dorothy walked across the floor she stumbled over the bar, not

220
00:17:50,908 --> 00:17:54,510
being able to see it, and fell at full length. She was

221
00:17:54,510 --> 00:17:59,340
not much hurt, but in her fall one of the Silver Shoes came off; and before she

222
00:17:59,340 --> 00:18:03,630
could reach it, the Witch had snatched it away and put it on her own

223
00:18:03,630 --> 00:18:08,279
skinny foot. The wicked woman was greatly pleased with the success of her trick,

224
00:18:08,279 --> 00:18:12,930
for as long as she had one of the shoes she owned half the power of their charm,

225
00:18:12,930 --> 00:18:19,260
and Dorothy could not use it against her, even had she known how to do so. The

226
00:18:19,260 --> 00:18:24,059
little girl, seeing she had lost one of her pretty shoes, grew angry, and said to the Witch,

227
00:18:24,059 --> 00:18:29,340
“Give me back my shoe!” “I will not,” retorted

228
00:18:29,340 --> 00:18:36,239
the Witch, “for it is now my shoe, and not yours.” “You are a wicked creature!” cried

229
00:18:36,239 --> 00:18:41,100
Dorothy. “You have no right to take my shoe from me.” “I shall keep it, just the

230
00:18:41,100 --> 00:18:47,389
same,” said the Witch, laughing at her, “and someday I shall get the other one from you, too.”

231
00:18:47,389 --> 00:18:52,350
This made Dorothy so very angry that she picked up the bucket of water that stood

232
00:18:52,350 --> 00:18:57,659
near and dashed it over the Witch, wetting her from head to foot. Instantly

233
00:18:57,659 --> 00:19:02,579
the wicked woman gave a loud cry of fear, and then, as Dorothy looked at her in

234
00:19:02,579 --> 00:19:07,860
wonder, the Witch began to shrink and fall away. “See what you have done!” she

235
00:19:07,860 --> 00:19:13,290
screamed. “In a minute I shall melt away.” “I’m very sorry, indeed,” said Dorothy, who

236
00:19:13,290 --> 00:19:16,979
was truly frightened to see the Witch actually melting away like brown sugar

237
00:19:16,979 --> 00:19:22,680
before her very eyes. “Didn’t you know water would be the end of me?” asked the

238
00:19:22,680 --> 00:19:27,420
Witch, in a wailing, despairing voice. “Of course not,” answered Dorothy.

239
00:19:27,420 --> 00:19:31,920
“How should I?” “Well, in a few minutes I shall be all melted, and you will have

240
00:19:31,920 --> 00:19:36,299
the castle to yourself. I have been wicked in my day, but I have never

241
00:19:36,299 --> 00:19:40,860
thought a little girl like you would ever be able to melt me and in my wicked

242
00:19:40,860 --> 00:19:47,100
deeds. Look out— —here I go!” With these words the Witch fell down in a brown,

243
00:19:47,100 --> 00:19:51,690
melted, shapeless mass and began to spread over the clean boards of the

244
00:19:51,690 --> 00:19:56,670
kitchen floor. Seeing that she had really melted away into nothing, Dorothy drew

245
00:19:56,670 --> 00:20:01,079
another bucket of water and threw it over the mess. She then swept it all out

246
00:20:01,079 --> 00:20:05,430
the door. After picking out the silver shoe, which was all it was left of the

247
00:20:05,430 --> 00:20:08,100
old woman, she cleaned and dried it with a cloth,

248
00:20:08,100 --> 00:20:11,159
and put it on her foot again. Then, being

249
00:20:11,159 --> 00:20:15,719
at last free to do as she chose, she ran out to the courtyard to tell the Lion

250
00:20:15,719 --> 00:20:19,680
that the Wicked Witch of the West had come to an end, and that they were no

251
00:20:19,680 --> 00:20:21,000
longer prisoners in a strange land
